1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,020
Press a key, and the board measures a voltage and prints it. Let's read every line.

2
00:00:07,020 --> 00:00:14,537
Open 03 adc reference. Your project needs the adc16 driver. Paste the lesson over

3
00:00:14,537 --> 00:00:18,620
your main file, and its pin file into board.

4
00:00:19,120 --> 00:00:26,744
A knob is optional. Unplug first, and ask your leader to check: outer legs to ground

5
00:00:26,744 --> 00:00:29,920
and 3.3 volts, middle leg to PTE30.

6
00:00:30,420 --> 00:00:31,860
Never 5 volts.

7
00:00:32,860 --> 00:00:39,660
Line 88: text in and out. Line 89: board details.

8
00:00:40,160 --> 00:00:45,920
Line 90 is new: the ADC driver, the analog to digital converter.

9
00:00:46,420 --> 00:00:52,100
Lines 92 and 93: pin and clock setup. Line 99 starts main.

10
00:00:53,100 --> 00:00:57,740
Switch on lines 120 to 122 with the comment shortcut.

11
00:00:58,240 --> 00:01:08,160
Line 120 sets pin jobs. Line 121 starts the heartbeat. Line 122 opens the text line.

12
00:01:08,660 --> 00:01:14,820
Switch on line 143. It prints a title so you know the program started.

13
00:01:15,820 --> 00:01:19,180
Switch on lines 180 to 194.

14
00:01:19,680 --> 00:01:23,840
Line 180 nicknames the converter, ADC zero.

15
00:01:24,340 --> 00:01:32,600
Line 181 picks setting group zero. Line 182 picks input channel 23, which is pin

16
00:01:32,600 --> 00:01:33,220
PTE30.

17
00:01:34,220 --> 00:01:37,580
Line 184 makes an empty settings form.

18
00:01:38,080 --> 00:01:42,080
Line 186 fills it with the driver's defaults.

19
00:01:42,580 --> 00:01:49,187
Lines 188 to 190: if this board asks for the other voltage reference, line 189

20
00:01:49,187 --> 00:01:54,100
selects it. The reference is the ruler we measure against.

21
00:01:54,600 --> 00:01:58,200
Line 192 gives the settings to the converter.

22
00:01:58,700 --> 00:02:07,500
Line 194, false, means no hardware trigger. A measurement starts when our code asks.

23
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:11,540
Switch on lines 221 to 230.

24
00:02:12,040 --> 00:02:17,799
Line 221 includes this block only if the chip supports calibration. Line 230 closes

25
00:02:17,799 --> 00:02:18,840
that condition.

26
00:02:19,340 --> 00:02:23,740
Line 222 runs the self test, and asks: did it succeed?

27
00:02:24,240 --> 00:02:32,160
If yes, line 224 prints Done. Otherwise line 228 prints Failed.

28
00:02:32,660 --> 00:02:35,620
Calibration is like zeroing a kitchen scale.

29
00:02:36,620 --> 00:02:40,540
Switch on lines 254 to 263.

30
00:02:41,040 --> 00:02:46,080
Line 254 makes a small form describing one measurement.

31
00:02:46,580 --> 00:02:49,620
Line 256 asks you to press a key.

32
00:02:50,120 --> 00:02:54,360
Line 258 writes channel 23 into the form.

33
00:02:54,860 --> 00:03:00,380
Line 259: no interrupt when finished. We'll wait for the answer ourselves.

34
00:03:00,880 --> 00:03:09,150
Lines 261 to 263 turn off two wire mode, if the chip has it. We measure one pin

35
00:03:09,150 --> 00:03:10,720
against ground.

36
00:03:11,720 --> 00:03:14,680
Switch on lines 300 to 315.

37
00:03:15,180 --> 00:03:18,380
Line 300: repeat forever.

38
00:03:18,880 --> 00:03:21,280
Line 302 waits for any key.

39
00:03:21,780 --> 00:03:27,822
Line 304 hands the channel form to the converter. In this mode, that starts the

40
00:03:27,822 --> 00:03:28,740
measurement.

41
00:03:29,240 --> 00:03:36,120
Lines 306 and 307 form a waiting loop: keep going while the done flag is still down.

42
00:03:36,620 --> 00:03:42,860
The empty brackets on lines 308 and 309 mean do nothing else.

43
00:03:43,360 --> 00:03:45,200
Waiting like this is called what?

44
00:03:45,700 --> 00:03:50,700
Your turn. Pause the video.

45
00:03:50,700 --> 00:03:51,340
Polling.

46
00:03:51,840 --> 00:03:56,960
Line 311 reads the raw number, from 0 to 4095.

47
00:03:57,460 --> 00:04:07,140
Line 312 changes it to millivolts: times 3300 first, then divided by 4095.

48
00:04:07,640 --> 00:04:14,360
Line 314 prints both. Percent u prints a number that is never negative.

49
00:04:15,360 --> 00:04:18,880
Save, build, load, and open your terminal. Press a key.

50
00:04:19,380 --> 00:04:23,985
These numbers are examples. Yours depend on the voltage on your pin. If the pin is

51
00:04:23,985 --> 00:04:25,220
empty, they'll wander.

52
00:04:26,220 --> 00:04:32,060
If raw is 1024, about a quarter of the range, what voltage?

53
00:04:32,560 --> 00:04:37,560
Your turn. Pause the video.

54
00:04:37,560 --> 00:04:39,800
About 825 millivolts.

55
00:04:40,300 --> 00:04:47,910
Try it the wrong way on line 312: divide first. Almost every reading becomes zero.

56
00:04:47,910 --> 00:04:49,580
Put the line back.

57
00:04:50,080 --> 00:04:55,360
Now you can explain how a chip turns a voltage into a number. Next demo: tilt.

